  "strategy": "### 1. Approach and Rationale\n\nWe propose using a semi-definite programming (SDP) relaxation combined with a structured local search to tackle the non-convexity of the overlap minimization problem. The key idea is to leverage the ability of SDPs to handle quadratic constraints more effectively than LPs, potentially yielding tighter bounds on C\u2085.\n\n### 2. Mathematical Specification\n\n**Variables and Objective**:\n- Let \\( h \\in \\mathbb{R}^n \\) with \\( n = n\\_points \\).\n- Minimize \\( C_5 = \\max_k \\sum_{i=0}^{n-k-1} h[i](1 - h[i+k]) \\cdot dx \\), where \\( dx = 2/n \\).\n- Constraints: \\( 0 \\leq h[i] \\leq 1 \\) and \\( \\sum h = n/2 \\).\n\n**SDP Relaxation**:\n- Introduce a matrix \\( Z \\) such that \\( Z = \\begin{bmatrix} I & H \\\\ H^T & I \\end{bmatrix} \\), where \\( H \\) is the matrix of \\( h[i]*(1 - h[j]) \\) for all i, j.\n- Enforce \\( Z \\succeq 0 \\) to capture quadratic constraints.\n\n### 3. Implementation Procedure\n\n1. **Initialization**:\n   - Start with `initial_h_values`, add small random perturbations, clip, and normalize.\n\n2. **SDP Setup**:\n   - Formulate SDP to minimize \\( t \\) subject to \\( Z \\succeq 0 \\) and \\( t \\geq \\) overlap for all lags.\n\n3. **Dual and Primal Updates**:\n   - Solve SDP, extract eigenvalues to compute h.\n   - Use gradient descent with adaptive steps for local refinement.\n\n4. **Feasibility Repair**:\n   - After each update, clip h to [0,1] and rescale to maintain integral.\n\n5. **Termination**:\n   - Stop at budget exhaustion or when target C5 achieved.\n\n### 4. Search Choices and Variations\n\n- **n_points**: 70 as per parent, for balance.\n- **SDP Solver**: Use MOSEK if available, else CVXOPT.\n- **Step Adaptation**: Initial step 0.01, decrease by 0.9 every 50 steps.\n\n### 5. Compute Budget and Robustness\n\n- **Time Estimate**: SDP solves take ~2-3 seconds each, with 200 iterations in budget.\n- **Fallback**: Use initial_h_values if SDP fails.\n\n### 6. Validation and Return\n\n- Ensure h satisfies constraints, recompute C5 with evaluator, return best found.",
